WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.050 --> 00:00:06.331
Jordan Peele's follow-up to his masterly debut
Get Out is a terrifying plunge into the double

00:00:06.331 --> 00:00:08.050
depths of humanity.

00:00:08.050 --> 00:00:11.940
And hidden in the movie's opening scenes and
throughout the film are a stack of clues that

00:00:11.940 --> 00:00:14.440
cleverly reveal its dramatic twist ending.

00:00:14.440 --> 00:00:19.070
Yippee-ki-yay, movie lovers, I'm Jan and today
I'm really excited to peel back and break

00:00:19.070 --> 00:00:22.939
down for you the many layers of the horrifying
and thrilling film Us.

00:00:22.939 --> 00:00:26.620
We've got tons of easter eggs you may have
missed and hidden secrets to examine; and

00:00:26.620 --> 00:00:30.519
there will, of course, be spoilers, so take
care if you haven't seen it yet.

00:00:30.519 --> 00:00:34.780
Let's quickly review what happened at the
end of Us so we understand exactly how all

00:00:34.780 --> 00:00:38.510
the clues in the movie foreshadow and explain
the final twist.

00:00:38.510 --> 00:00:43.210
The Tethered were originally created as part
of an experiment to control their above-ground

00:00:43.210 --> 00:00:44.210
counterparts.

00:00:44.210 --> 00:00:48.250
When that failed, the doubles were abandoned
in their underground world of tunnels.

00:00:48.250 --> 00:00:53.140
But Red, Adelaide's double, helps the Tethered
rise up against the above-ground people, killing

00:00:53.140 --> 00:00:56.280
many of them and forming a human chain across
the land.

00:00:56.280 --> 00:01:01.320
However, the twist we discover is that the
Tethered's leader Red is actually the original

00:01:01.320 --> 00:01:02.320
Adelaide.

00:01:02.320 --> 00:01:07.950
In the 1980s fairground scene, young Adelaide
encountered her double in a hall of mirrors.

00:01:07.950 --> 00:01:12.660
Adelaide's double overpowered her and took
Adelaide's place with her parents above ground.

00:01:12.660 --> 00:01:16.560
In the final showdown, present-day Adelaide
kills Red burying underground forever the

00:01:16.560 --> 00:01:22.270
truth about who she really is, or at least
that's what she thinks.

00:01:22.270 --> 00:01:25.990
Because her son Jason was also in the tunnels
when Red revealed that present-day Adelaide

00:01:25.990 --> 00:01:31.320
was originally one of the Tethered, and it
seems like he may have overheard her secret.

00:01:31.320 --> 00:01:35.430
Which is why he gives her a sort of strange
look and pulls down his mask as they drive

00:01:35.430 --> 00:01:37.020
off in the final scene.

00:01:37.020 --> 00:01:41.140
So, now we've got the ending covered, let's
jump back to the movie's opening scenes where

00:01:41.140 --> 00:01:45.340
Jordan Peele laid clues for everything in
the ending and the twist.

00:01:45.340 --> 00:01:49.480
The movie starts with Young Adelaide watching
a Hands Across America commercial, which is

00:01:49.480 --> 00:01:53.040
full of imagery and themes we'll see repeated
during the film.

00:01:53.040 --> 00:01:57.900
The idea of doubles and twins is immediately
brought up with the mention of 6 million people

00:01:57.900 --> 00:02:03.430
and 12 million eyes, the Twin Towers, the
repeated letter Gs of the Golden Gate bridge,

00:02:03.430 --> 00:02:08.560
and the words 'from sea to shining sea' accompany
an image of a lighthouse which appears twice,

00:02:08.560 --> 00:02:12.700
once on the left of the screen and then immediately
flipped with a mirror image to the right;

00:02:12.700 --> 00:02:17.760
and to cap it all, after the promo ends, we
see a copy of Young Adelaide reflected in

00:02:17.760 --> 00:02:20.990
the TV screen, sitting next to a white rabbit
toy.

00:02:20.990 --> 00:02:26.150
Now, that's intriguing because the advert
is a huge clue to the real identity of the

00:02:26.150 --> 00:02:27.150
Tethered's leader.

00:02:27.150 --> 00:02:31.620
When Young Adelaide is switched with her tethered
counterpart, her tethered double steals her

00:02:31.620 --> 00:02:36.640
Michael Jackson Thriller T-shirt and leaves
her only with her Hands Across America T-shirt.

00:02:36.640 --> 00:02:42.290
Over the years she spent growing up underground,
Adelaide, who we now call Red, found inspiration

00:02:42.290 --> 00:02:44.980
in memories from her previous life above ground.

00:02:44.980 --> 00:02:50.010
Her memory of that 1986 Hands Across America
charity event during which millions of Americans

00:02:50.010 --> 00:02:54.200
formed a human chain across parts of the country
to raise money for the homeless is something

00:02:54.200 --> 00:02:56.180
that's been seared into her brain.

00:02:56.180 --> 00:03:00.970
And so Red takes inspiration from that to
organise an act of rebellion where the Tethered

00:03:00.970 --> 00:03:05.900
send a bloody message by rising up, killing
their above-ground counterparts, and recreating

00:03:05.900 --> 00:03:08.990
that human chain from Hands Across America.

00:03:08.990 --> 00:03:13.680
The clothes worn by the Tethered give their
group the feeling of a cult or escaped prisoners,

00:03:13.680 --> 00:03:15.210
which in a way they are.

00:03:15.210 --> 00:03:19.490
And Red, who designed these outfits, took
inspiration from several classic horror and

00:03:19.490 --> 00:03:24.200
cultural icons from the time prior to 1986
when she was still above ground.

00:03:24.200 --> 00:03:28.349
The red work-wear jumpsuits and the single
gloves worn by the doppelgangers are a nod

00:03:28.349 --> 00:03:33.069
to Michael Jackson's Thriller video as well
as his custom of donning a glove on just one

00:03:33.069 --> 00:03:34.069
hand.

00:03:34.069 --> 00:03:37.640
Their outfits also evoke Michael Myers' overalls
in the Halloween movies.

00:03:37.640 --> 00:03:41.620
And, likewise, their gloves are a shout-out
to Freddie Krueger's look in the Nightmare

00:03:41.620 --> 00:03:46.709
on Elm Street series, a VHS tape of which
you see by the TV set at the start of Peele's

00:03:46.709 --> 00:03:50.500
film, which is doubly fitting as it's one
of the director's favourite horrors.

00:03:50.500 --> 00:03:56.020
Another of those VHS tapes by the TV is the
mid-80s horror film C.H.U.D., which hints

00:03:56.020 --> 00:04:00.870
nicely at how Us will end because as well
as featuring monsters living beneath the streets

00:04:00.870 --> 00:04:06.360
who eventually emerge above ground, C.H.U.D.
also includes a government cover-up and, interestingly

00:04:06.360 --> 00:04:09.349
for Peele's movie, people who go missing.

00:04:09.349 --> 00:04:14.331
By the way, the idea of conspiracies also
pops up early on in Us when Zora says that

00:04:14.331 --> 00:04:17.840
the government uses fluoride in water to control
people's minds.

00:04:17.840 --> 00:04:22.430
Not only is this a pointer to the failed experiment
revealed at the end of the movie, but it may

00:04:22.430 --> 00:04:27.360
also be a little shout-out to the movie Dr
Strangelove, especially given Jordan Peele's

00:04:27.360 --> 00:04:30.590
love of Stanley Kubrick films like The Shining.

00:04:30.590 --> 00:04:34.960
Another key detail to understand the film's
ending is the constant repetition of the number

00:04:34.960 --> 00:04:35.960
11.

00:04:35.960 --> 00:04:40.249
In the very first scene, there's the news
and weather promo, "all on 7 at 11".

00:04:40.249 --> 00:04:44.650
There's the prize young Adelaide choses at
the fairground, "I want number 11", she tells

00:04:44.650 --> 00:04:45.650
her dad.

00:04:45.650 --> 00:04:49.960
There's the alarm clock that hits 11 minutes
past 11 shortly before the Wilsons' doubles

00:04:49.960 --> 00:04:51.849
turn up and wreak havoc.

00:04:51.849 --> 00:04:56.050
And those same numbers are on the roof of
the ambulance as it drives away at the end.

00:04:56.050 --> 00:05:02.169
And, importantly, a man with a "Jeremiah 11:11"
sign pops up by the beach in the past and

00:05:02.169 --> 00:05:03.279
the present.

00:05:03.279 --> 00:05:08.189
The man with the sign is a harbinger of doom,
as you can tell from his Biblical verse: "Therefore

00:05:08.189 --> 00:05:12.559
this is what the Lord says: 'I will bring
on them a disaster they cannot escape.

00:05:12.559 --> 00:05:15.659
Although they cry out to me, I will not listen
to them."

00:05:15.659 --> 00:05:20.789
This Biblical warning precedes Adelaide's
traumatic experiences in both the past and

00:05:20.789 --> 00:05:21.819
the present.

00:05:21.819 --> 00:05:25.349
What's intriguing for this movie though is
that this particular chapter of the Bible

00:05:25.349 --> 00:05:29.889
is all about how God's people have forgotten
their own history, where they come from, and

00:05:29.889 --> 00:05:32.711
the commitment and promises they made to get
to where they are.

00:05:32.711 --> 00:05:37.849
All of which ties in nicely with the way Adelaide
tries to bury the memory of what happened

00:05:37.849 --> 00:05:42.259
in 1986 as she wants to forget how she ended
up where she is today.

00:05:42.259 --> 00:05:47.199
In other words, she's desperately trying to
avoid confronting the fact that she's a doppelganger

00:05:47.199 --> 00:05:52.319
who used violence to replace the original
Adelaide and now she's terrified what consequences

00:05:52.319 --> 00:05:56.650
her actions back then might have for herself
and her family today if she returns to the

00:05:56.650 --> 00:05:58.689
scene of her original crime.

00:05:58.689 --> 00:06:04.009
The film's frequently repeated Bible quote
is a stark message that goes out to its protagonists

00:06:04.009 --> 00:06:08.819
and viewers in general, and also, given the
double meaning of the film's title, specifically

00:06:08.819 --> 00:06:10.199
to the US.

00:06:10.199 --> 00:06:14.669
When Gabe asks his family's doppelgangers,
"What are you people?", the question word

00:06:14.669 --> 00:06:20.449
"what" implies a certain dehumanisation as
Gabe doesn't quite see the doubles as human

00:06:20.449 --> 00:06:21.449
beings.

00:06:21.449 --> 00:06:26.509
Red's answer, "We're Americans", is unnerving
for the Wilsons who see the strange but familiar

00:06:26.509 --> 00:06:30.300
beings in front of them as different, not
equal to themselves.

00:06:30.300 --> 00:06:35.719
As a visual double of the number 1, the number
11 perfectly suits the film's imagery and

00:06:35.719 --> 00:06:38.610
themes of doubling, mirroring, and repetition.

00:06:38.610 --> 00:06:42.950
But through the ages, the number 11 has also
acquired some interesting symbolism as it

00:06:42.950 --> 00:06:48.669
can signify "transition, excess, peril and
conflict", all of which there's plenty of

00:06:48.669 --> 00:06:49.669
in this film.

00:06:49.669 --> 00:06:54.889
And it can also symbolise "sin and repentance",
which seems very fitting given this movie

00:06:54.889 --> 00:06:59.410
suggests we need to take a long, hard look
at ourselves to find the source of our individual

00:06:59.410 --> 00:07:03.699
and collective problems rather than constantly
be rushing to blame other people or groups

00:07:03.699 --> 00:07:05.969
for our self-inflicted disasters.

00:07:05.969 --> 00:07:10.189
That is, we are our own worst enemies.

00:07:10.189 --> 00:07:13.729
The owl that pops out and frightens Young
Adelaide at the fair fits in with the murals

00:07:13.729 --> 00:07:15.770
of trees and wildlife around her.

00:07:15.770 --> 00:07:20.539
But given the setting is a Vision Quest, with
a sign to 'Find Yourself', the bird's symbolism

00:07:20.539 --> 00:07:22.719
is also interesting for the film.

00:07:22.719 --> 00:07:28.060
In some symbolism, owls are connected to magical
forces and the idea of being able to see beyond

00:07:28.060 --> 00:07:32.210
your immediate reality, something the film
seems to implore us to do.

00:07:32.210 --> 00:07:37.059
And it's also intriguing that, symbolically,
owls embody the duality seen in the film as

00:07:37.059 --> 00:07:41.500
they can be considered a good or a bad omen,
depending on what happens next.

00:07:41.500 --> 00:07:45.440
Clearly, for young Adelaide, it was a bad
omen, though things worked out better for

00:07:45.440 --> 00:07:49.479
her doppelganger who got to experience life
above ground.

00:07:49.479 --> 00:07:53.710
Inside the hall of mirrors, Young Adelaide
starts whistling the tune to the nursery rhyme

00:07:53.710 --> 00:07:58.159
Itsy Bitsy Spider, but then her clone takes
over, whistling it too.

00:07:58.159 --> 00:08:03.270
The nursery rhyme is about a spider climbing
up a drainpipe before being flushed down again

00:08:03.270 --> 00:08:04.379
when it rains.

00:08:04.379 --> 00:08:08.349
And then when the sun comes out and dries
away the rain, the spider is able to finally

00:08:08.349 --> 00:08:09.940
emerge from below.

00:08:09.940 --> 00:08:14.990
The lyrics match exactly how young Adelaide's
clone comes up to the surface, then drags

00:08:14.990 --> 00:08:19.330
Adelaide back down below, before returning
to the surface to take young Adelaide's place

00:08:19.330 --> 00:08:21.330
in the world above ground.

00:08:21.330 --> 00:08:26.180
Years later Red finally has her moment and
comes up to the surface for the Untethering.

00:08:26.180 --> 00:08:30.110
And that final emergence from below by Red
is foreshadowed in the scene at the holiday

00:08:30.110 --> 00:08:35.199
home where present-day Adelaide spots a tiny
little spider creeping out from below a bigger

00:08:35.199 --> 00:08:37.599
toy spider on the coffee table.

00:08:37.599 --> 00:08:42.440
The cages of rabbits gradually revealed during
the film's opening credits are another fascinating

00:08:42.440 --> 00:08:45.350
detail that point to the film's final twist.

00:08:45.350 --> 00:08:49.829
Peele moves quickly from an image of young
Adelaide's horrified face at seeing her double

00:08:49.829 --> 00:08:54.670
for the first time and cuts straight to a
close-up of a caged rabbit's face, indicating

00:08:54.670 --> 00:08:59.390
young Adelaide's ultimate fate of being caged
underground with the Tethered, who, like rabbits,

00:08:59.390 --> 00:09:02.630
have been living in tunnels and were used
for experiments.

00:09:02.630 --> 00:09:06.839
The flashback to Young Adelaide and her parents
at the doctors after her traumatic hall of

00:09:06.839 --> 00:09:11.019
mirrors experience is another vital clue to
the film's body swap twist.

00:09:11.019 --> 00:09:15.449
Two details are particularly crucial: first,
Adelaide has been acting differently to her

00:09:15.449 --> 00:09:17.779
usual self and she doesn't speak.

00:09:17.779 --> 00:09:21.850
And secondly, there's her mother's words:
'I just want my little girl back.'

00:09:21.850 --> 00:09:26.630
A few scenes later we learn that the Wilson
family's doubles do not speak, which explains

00:09:26.630 --> 00:09:29.910
why young Adelaide couldn't speak after the
funfair.

00:09:29.910 --> 00:09:34.440
And the fact that only present-day Adelaide's
doppelganger can speak but sounds like someone

00:09:34.440 --> 00:09:38.990
who hasn't used her vocal chords in an extremely
long time is further evidence of the hall

00:09:38.990 --> 00:09:40.830
of mirrors body swap.

00:09:40.830 --> 00:09:44.690
Young Adelaide's mum didn't realise, but she
never did get her 'little girl back' as the

00:09:44.690 --> 00:09:49.490
child living with her after that day in Santa
Cruz was actually her daughter's clone.

00:09:49.490 --> 00:09:53.519
This hint is echoed in the present day when
Adelaide tries to tell her husband Gabe why

00:09:53.519 --> 00:09:58.829
she feels so uncomfortable on holiday, 'I
don't feel like myself', she says; but just

00:09:58.829 --> 00:10:03.589
like Young Adelaide's parents, Gabe doesn't
know or understand what that really means,

00:10:03.589 --> 00:10:06.449
and replies, 'I think you look like yourself.'

00:10:06.449 --> 00:10:10.759
Then there's the way, later during the home
invasion, that Red orders Adelaide, 'tether

00:10:10.759 --> 00:10:14.769
yourself to the table', an act which mirrors
how Young Adelaide's doppelanger chained her

00:10:14.769 --> 00:10:18.860
to the bed in the underground tunnels before
leaving her there and replacing her above

00:10:18.860 --> 00:10:19.860
ground.

00:10:19.860 --> 00:10:22.600
Of course, there are other signs that things
are not all they appear to be.

00:10:22.600 --> 00:10:27.230
For example, in the background here, there's
the board game 'Guess Who?', and notice that

00:10:27.230 --> 00:10:31.800
other game, 'Magic', is actually turned upside-down,
much like Adelaide's life has been since the

00:10:31.800 --> 00:10:34.620
hall of mirrors encounter with her double.

00:10:34.620 --> 00:10:38.850
Peele's film begins by showing us how we like
to think of ourselves – the good Samaritans

00:10:38.850 --> 00:10:43.339
of the Hands Across America promo – and
ends by exposing our shadowy side which we

00:10:43.339 --> 00:10:47.779
so often prefer to keep hidden both from ourselves
and the rest of the world.

00:10:47.779 --> 00:10:52.920
Essentially, Us is a film about society's
hypocrisy, the everyday violence of our privileges

00:10:52.920 --> 00:10:58.500
and power, as well as issues such as class,
race, xenophobia, and general fear of the

00:10:58.500 --> 00:11:03.190
other, as well as the way so many choose to
ignore the ties that bind us and look instead

00:11:03.190 --> 00:11:08.421
to sow seeds of division, keeping others down
in order to raise themselves up, in a constant

00:11:08.421 --> 00:11:13.370
game of Whac-a-mole, a game we see Young Adelaide's
dad playing as she wanders off, soon to be

00:11:13.370 --> 00:11:15.310
lost to her parents forever.

00:11:15.310 --> 00:11:18.209
Now, did you catch any other details in Us?

00:11:18.209 --> 00:11:20.500
And do you have any theories about the ending?

00:11:20.500 --> 00:11:22.470
Let me know in the comments below!

00:11:22.470 --> 00:11:26.860
Coming up I'll have new videos on Pet Sematary
and Chilling Adventures of Sabrina, so if

00:11:26.860 --> 00:11:31.370
you enjoy deep-dives into your favourite horrors,
